---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import register from "../data/register.json";

const rows = register.filter((r) => ["MAIONE", "MAJONE"].includes((r.surname || "").toUpperCase()));
const swept = rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "swept").length;
---
<Base title="Maione"
      eyebrow="Generation six married a Maione"
      dek="Costanza Maione of Arienzo — the mother of Carminantonio, and the one wife in the spine whose own birth record has actually been found.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>Maione is the second maternal line this archive left unwritten.</strong> Like
    <a href={u("/annecchino")}>Annecchino</a> it belongs to the direct line, not to the margins:
    <strong>Costanza Maione married Raffaele Falco</strong>, and their son Carminantonio carried the
    name to Australia. The archive held {rows.length} Maione and Majone records and gave the family
    no page.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Costanza Maione</h2>
  <p class="lede">Born at <strong>Arienzo on 2 May 1876</strong>. She married <P n="Raffaele Falco" bold={true} />, born at Arienzo 6 April 1873, and the children the archive can name are
  <strong>Giovanna</strong> (1898), <strong>Carminantonio</strong> (1901), <P n="Anna del Giudice" bold={true} /> (1904), <strong>Gerardo</strong> (1913), and <strong>Giacoma</strong> and
  <strong>Aniello</strong>, undated.</p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
    <p><strong>Hers is the one wife's birth in the spine with a record behind it.</strong> Filomena
    Annecchino's birth is known only from the family tree; Costanza's is
    <strong>cited</strong> — a Caserta <em>Stato Civile</em> entry of 2 May 1876 which also names her
    father, <P n="Aniello Maione" bold={true} />. That is why this page can say more about 1876 than the
    Annecchino page can about 1897.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Her household at Arienzo</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Born</th><th>What the archive has</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Aniello Maione / Majone</strong>, her father</td><td class="mono">1841, Arienzo</td>
        <td>Named in Costanza's own birth entry, and in the tree.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Anna Martone" bold={true} />, her mother</td><td class="mono">1846, Arienzo</td>
        <td>The tree. <strong>Martone is one of the commonest Arienzo names in these registers</strong> —
        375 records — so she is not yet joined to any Martone household.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Costanza Maione" bold={true} /></td><td class="mono">2 May 1876, Arienzo</td>
        <td><strong>Cited record.</strong> Married Raffaele Falco.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Michel'angelo Maione, her brother</td><td class="mono">10 Mar 1881, Arienzo</td><td>The tree.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Maria Maione, her sister</td><td class="mono">1 Dec 1884, Arienzo</td><td>The tree.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>One spelling, three shapes</h2>
  <p>The family is written <strong>Maione</strong> in the twentieth-century papers and
  <strong>Majone</strong> throughout the nineteenth-century Arienzo registers — the same name with the
  older orthography. A third form, <strong>Maggiore</strong>, is a <em>different</em> Arienzo family
  and is not merged here, although the hands of the 1820s and 1840s make the two look alike. The rule
  this archive follows for that kind of pair is set out under <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>

  <h2>The Majone on the ground, 1816–1843</h2>
  <p>{swept} of these records were read off the Arienzo death registers line by line rather than
  found in an index. They put the family in specific streets, in specific trades:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><P n="Don Raffaele Majone" bold={true} /></td><td>Died at <strong>eighty-four</strong>,
        <em>«di professione SACERDOTE»</em> — a priest, son of Tomaso Majone. <strong>The family put a
        son into the Church.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Francesco Majone" bold={true} /></td><td>Forty-five, <em>«massaro di campagna»</em> —
        a farm overseer, not a day labourer. The same rank the register gives Giuseppe Falco in 1817.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Celestino Majone" bold={true} /></td><td>Forty-six, <em>sartore</em>, declaring at the
        death of a <em>gentildonna</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Gennaro, Giuseppe, Filippo Majone</strong></td><td><em>Contadini</em> and
        <em>bracciali</em> of <strong>strada Cossi / Cioffi</strong>, strada Caldarola and strada
        Capodiconca, declaring across the 1820s to the 1840s.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Gaspare Majone" bold={true} /></td><td>Twenty-three in 1840 on <strong>strada
        Costa</strong>, declaring twice in five days; thirty-four by the December of that year.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><P n="Antonio Majone" bold={true} /></td><td>Given as thirty-two, thirty-six, forty-five and
        fifty in four different acts — <em>the age problem, in one family</em>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p class="small"><strong>None of these has been joined to Aniello Maione of 1841.</strong> They are
  the family in its town, forty years before Costanza was born, and they are published as that rather
  than as a pedigree.</p>

  <h2>What would move this line</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Aniello Maione's birth act, about 1841, Arienzo.</strong> It would name his parents and
    reach back into the swept Majone above — several of whom are the right age to be his father.</li>
    <li><strong>The marriage of Aniello Maione and Anna Martone</strong>, about 1865–1875. It names
    both fathers.</li>
    <li><strong>The marriage of Raffaele Falco and Costanza Maione</strong>, about 1895. It would tie
    the two families together in one document and name four parents at once.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">All {rows.length} Maione and Majone records are listed with
  their sources at <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Majone"}>the register →</a></p>
</Base>
